dell has just updated their XPS 13  laptop for 2019 and we got an  entry-level I 5 model for $1,150 which  is a great price considering it packs  eight gigabytes of RAM and 256 gigs of  nvme SSD storage so let's go through  everything you need to know about it  just like last year its encased in an  aluminum shell that looks and feels  great compared to many other Windows  laptops the display has very thin  great-looking bezels despite the fact  that they've moved the webcam back up  above the display last year's model had  the webcam under the display to  accommodate Windows hello a useful  feature that scans your face to log you  in without having to enter your password  that feature is now gone  but it's not a big deal since the power  button doubles as a fingerprint scanner  for logging in the display itself uses a  1080p panel that definitely isn't the  sharpest display out there but the  excellent color accuracy in the matte  filter make it one of the better 1080p  displays you can find but either way if  you care a lot about the display we'd  recommend upgrading to the 4k model for  a few hundred dollars more the keyboard  feels pretty decent for a Windows laptop  with a good amount of key travel typing  is pretty comfortable but some users did  complain that there was a bit too much  spacing between the keys personally we  prefer the latest MacBook Pro keys which  are wider and more clicky  but most people prefer the Dells it also  features backlighting with two  brightness settings so it's great for  using at night the build quality is  really solid and doesn't flex like other  cheaper Windows laptops do one thing we  can complain about is that there's no  notch in the front to help open the  laptop so you kind of have to jam your  fingernail in there to open it up and  the hinge itself is pretty stiff so it's  tough to open with one hand the trackpad  is also pretty good for a Windows laptop  but you definitely won't get the same  experience as with one of the newer Mac  books which feature the best track pads  in the business but the XPS 13 trackpad  is still above average one thing that  really stands out about the new XPS 13  is that it packs a total of three full  speed USB seaports with two of them  supporting full speed Thunderbolt 3 a  lot of other Windows laptops only come  with one full speed Thunderbolt 3 port  or two slower ones so having two full  speed ports is impressive it also packs  a mini SD card slot a headphone jack in  a neat little battery indicator that  shows you your current battery life one  thing that still disappoints us  year-over-year is the speaker quality it  sounds ok but it  definitely lacks volume in a wide range  of frequency reproduction  [Music]  a few years ago these types of speakers  were the norm but many windows Aptos  have been making big improvements the  720p webcam actually isn't too bad using  noise reduction to make for a clearer  but not a sharp image but the microphone  quality was really terrible just listen  for yourself this is a microphone  quality tests on the Dell XPS 13 this is  a microphone quality test on the MacBook  Pro on the plus side  battery life is also really impressive  compared to last year but that's using  the default battery saving settings if  you leave it on these settings you'll  get decent performance similar to the  base 13 inch dual-core MacBook Pro which  is not what you'd expect from its I 5  quad-core processor but if you turn on  best performance mode you'll gain a lot  of extra processing power and we saw the  same results for the graphics as well  compared to last year we're getting a  ton of graphics performance this is  probably one of the biggest standout  features of this laptop it's really  powerful for the price so can handle  things like entry-level video or photo  editing really well and even simple 4k  video editing however we're a bit  disappointed with the nvme storage speed  it's not too bad but we really hope  it'll speed up next year but either way  if you're someone who's looking for a  good quality laptop with great  performance for around the $1000 range  the Dell XPS 13 is a good choice it's a  great laptop overall with only a couple  of downsides that many regular users may  not mind and at a price of just $1,150  it's a really good value but if your  sewin will be using the soft top in a  bright environment or watching a lot of  video content and music you might want  to look elsewhere huawei mate book X pro  would be a great alternative but with  everything that's been going on with  huawei recently it's a tough sell if  you've got some extra cash definitely  look into the 4k model of the XPS 13 or  maybe even one of the higher-end XPS 15  models which packs dedicated graphics  and six core processors if you're also  considering buying an Apple MacBook you  should definitely check out our  comparison between the XPS 13 and the  base 13 inch MacBook Pro right there  make sure to smash that like button and  click the circle above to subscribe  thanks for watching and we'll see you in  the next video  [Music]
 

